# T7-005 -- restacking without changing the layer set
#
# Three layers stay exactly where they are on screen; only which one is on
# top changes. Nothing is added, nothing is removed, no geometry moves.
#
# On a card whose planes carry a settable zpos this is two property writes
# and the layers stay where they are. On a card whose planes do not -- vkms,
# and plenty of embedded display controllers -- stacking *is* plane index, so
# the only way to restack is to move the layers between planes, and the whole
# assignment has to be rebuilt bottom-up.
#
# The two behaviors look completely different in a trace, which is the point:
# whichever the driver forces, both implementations have to reach the same
# one. A port that got the plane order right on the initial assignment can
# still get it wrong on a restack, because a restack is the case where the
# warm-start cache holds an assignment that is now in the wrong order -- and
# reusing it is exactly what warm-start is for.
#
# Until drm-cxx 03bc1d7 both implementations did nothing here: the restack
# defeated the FB-only fast path, warm-start re-tested the cached assignment,
# the kernel accepted it -- still *valid*, just no longer in zpos order -- and
# the layers stayed on their planes. That was drm-cxx#239, filed from this
# scenario. Both now check plane order on the warm path and move the layers,
# and the two traces are byte-identical again with the fix.

add bottom 0 0 0 0 0
add mid    1 64 64 256 256
add top    2 400 200 256 256

frame
frame

# Send the top layer to the back and promote the middle one.
zpos top 0
zpos bottom 1
zpos mid 2
frame
frame
